<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  F27-T11 · Android 12+ (API 31+) backup and transfer rules: nothing leaves.

  `android:allowBackup="false"` on <application> is the whole answer on
  Android 11 and lower. It is NOT the whole answer here: the app targets
  API 36, and for apps targeting API 31+ `allowBackup="false"` stops
  cloud backup (Google Drive) while, on devices from some manufacturers,
  leaving device-to-device transfer enabled. Only these rules stop that,
  so both the attribute and this file are needed.

  Why off completely (Q20, H2 — owner's decision, 2026-10-06):

  1. The local database is the real document archive. Drift/SQLite holds
     the extracted text of every paper the user analysed — names, amounts,
     account numbers, court dates — plus their reminders. Backing it up
     copies all of it to the user's Google account, which no part of the
     privacy screen prepares them for.
  2. A restored backup would be broken anyway. Saved images are encrypted
     with AES-256-GCM under a key held in `flutter_secure_storage`, which
     is backed by the Android Keystore. Keystore material does not leave
     the device, so a restore brings back ciphertext with no key: the
     images become permanently unreadable, and the database rows point at
     files that can never be opened again. Off is both the private answer
     and the correct one.

  Every domain is excluded explicitly. These sections default to
  INCLUDING everything not named, so a missing domain is a leak, not a
  no-op — hence all nine, in both sections, rather than `root` alone.

  `<cross-platform-transfer>` (Android 16 QPR2+, Android -> iOS) is
  deliberately absent: it has no counterpart app to receive anything
  while iOS is out of scope (Q1), and an element the older parsers on
  Android 12-15 do not recognise risks invalidating this whole file —
  which would silently re-enable what it is here to prevent. Add it in
  the task that ships iOS (T23/T24), on a device that can be tested.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <!-- Google Drive backup. -->
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <!-- Device-to-device transfer (new phone setup). Same answer: the
         encryption key cannot come with it, so transferred data would be
         unreadable even if the privacy question did not settle it. -->
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
        <exclude domain="device_root" />
        <exclude domain="device_file" />
        <exclude domain="device_database" />
        <exclude domain="device_sharedpref" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
